<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#libyco-berber-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Libyco-Berber script</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Libyc script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Libyco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> script is an ancient alphabet of consonants used to write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> from at least the third century BCE. It is known from thousands of funerary and rock inscriptions, and bilingual Punic texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dougga">Dougga</a>, including a dedication to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, made its partial decipherment possible. It is the ancestor of the Tifinagh script of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/libyco-berber-script.jpg" alt="Libyco-Berber script" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prehistory-draa16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the script is still debated. The leading theories see it either as a heavily modified form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a> or as a local invention influenced by it, and the view with most support is that it grew from a local prototype conceptually inspired by a Phoenician or archaic Semitic model; Greek, Punic or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> origins are thought unlikely. It is also called the Numidian script or the Old Libyan script, names meant to avoid assuming that Numidian continues into any surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The script was a pure abjad with no distinct vowels, though it had signs for w and y, and h may also have served for a. Doubled consonants were not marked. It was usually written from bottom to top, though right to left and other directions also occur, and its highly geometric letters took different forms when written vertically and horizontally. Most surviving inscriptions are simple funerary texts, beside rock and cave art, graffiti and a few official and possibly religious inscriptions.</p><p>Scholars distinguish an eastern and a western variant, or by another count more than 25 local forms in five families. The eastern variant, used around Constantine and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and to an extent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a>, is the best understood; the western, used along the coast from Kabylia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, had 13 additional letters. In the north the script died out during or after Roman and Byzantine rule, but it spread south into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and developed into the Tifinagh still used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Inscriptions are known in their thousands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, with a later Saharan form on rocks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. Among the most significant are those of the Massinissa Temple, discovered in 1904, and the Prince Ateban Mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dougga">Dougga</a>, as well as Azib N'Ikkis and Oukaimeden in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a>. Bilingual Numidian inscriptions in Libyco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Punic have allowed 22 of the eastern variant's 24 letters to be deciphered since 1843, while much of the western variant remained undeciphered in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Libyco-Berber alphabet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…kingdom's towns, among them Cirta, Thugga (Dougga), Zama Regia, Bulla Regia, Hippo Regius and Sicca, had strong Punic influence, and Punic was the main written language of court and administration, alongside the <b>Libyco-Berber script</b>, used in the bilingual dedication to Masinissa set up at Thugga in 139 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They speak the Tuareg languages, Tamasheq, Tamahaq and Tamajeq, of the Berber branch of the Afroasiatic family, and write them in Tifinagh, the old <b>Libyco-Berber script</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
